{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S2 — Capability decisions read `ModelProfile`, not provider-name strings\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nTwo capability decisions are made by string-matching a provider name. `src/rebar/llm/structured.py:63`\n`output_mode()` picks `NativeOutput` only when the prefix is in `_NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS`\n(`structured.py:46`, a frozenset of four literal names), and `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:341` attaches prompt-cache\nsettings only when the resolved string starts with `anthropic` (via `anthropic_model._anthropic_cache_settings`,\n`anthropic_model.py:39`). Both are wrong for Bedrock-hosted Claude, whose capability flags differ from the\ndirect Anthropic API — the model string would say `bedrock`, so caching would silently switch off and\nstructured output would silently take the prompted path. PR #121 hit exactly this and patched it with a\n`force_prompted: bool` threaded through four call sites.\n\n## Approach\n\npydantic-ai exposes a per-vendor capability record at `model.profile` (`ModelProfile`). Replace both string\nmatches with profile reads, and add a rebar-side override layer for cases upstream gets wrong or does not\ncover — using `ModelProfile` overrides for Bedrock-hosted Anthropic is the documented upstream guidance\nprecisely because those flags diverge from the direct API.\n\n### Where the code lives (resolves the S1 `providers.py` size conflict)\n\n`capabilities_for()` and the override table go in a **new leaf module `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py`**, NOT in\n`providers.py`. Reason: S1 (`7468-25e5-ae4f-43d1`, closed) carries an **attested** acceptance criterion that\n`providers.py` stays under 300 LOC, and it is already 225 LOC — adding the record, the override table and the\ncache-settings selector would breach a criterion that has been signed. Capability *interpretation* is also a\ndistinct concern from provider *construction*, and the repo's module-size policy targets 200–500 LOC units\nsplit along existing seams. `capabilities.py` is a leaf on the same footing as `anthropic_model.py`: it imports\nnothing from `runner` or `providers`, and heavy libraries are imported inside functions.\n\n### The capability record\n\n`capabilities_for(model) -> ModelCapabilities` returns a small frozen dataclass with **three** fields:\n\n- `native_structured_output: bool`\n- `prompt_cache_style: str` — one of `\"none\"` / `\"anthropic\"` / `\"bedrock\"`\n- `supports_thinking: bool`\n\n**The exact `ModelProfile` fields read** (verified against the pinned pydantic-ai 1.107.1):\n\n- `native_structured_output` is derived from `ModelProfile.supports_json_schema_output` (the strict\n  json_schema capability `NativeOutput` requires). `ModelProfile.supports_json_object_output` and\n  `ModelProfile.default_structured_output_mode` also exist and are NOT used — named here so a reader knows the\n  choice was made, not missed.\n- `prompt_cache_style` is derived from the profile's concrete type, not from a name string: a\n  `BedrockModelProfile` (which declares `bedrock_supports_prompt_caching` and `bedrock_supports_tool_caching`)\n  yields `\"bedrock\"`; an Anthropic profile yields `\"anthropic\"`; anything else yields `\"none\"`.\n- `supports_thinking` is derived from `ModelProfile.supports_thinking`. (`ModelProfile.thinking_always_enabled`\n  and `thinking_tags` also exist and are not read here — the record answers \"can this model reason at all\",\n  not how its reasoning is delimited.)\n\n### How the profile is obtained — the lazy-model-string problem S1 created\n\n`capabilities_for()` cannot simply read `model.profile`, because after S1 (`7468`, closed) **`run()` does not\nalways hold a model OBJECT.** S1 deliberately leaves a provider that pydantic-ai recognizes but rebar does not\nbuild (openai, google) as a lazy model **string**, because eagerly resolving it would force that vendor's\noptional SDK to be importable — `pydantic_ai.providers.openai` raises `ImportError: Please install the openai\npackage` and rebar's `[agents]` extra deliberately does not install it (verified: the `openai`, `google` and\n`groq` SDKs are all absent from the venv).\n\nSo `getattr(model, \"profile\", None)` on a `str` returns `None`, which would hit the conservative fallback and\nset `native_structured_output=False` for OpenAI and Google — silently destroying their `NativeOutput` path.\n`tests/unit/test_structured.py:177` asserts against exactly that, so it would surface as a regression.\n\n`capabilities_for(model_or_model_string)` therefore accepts **either** form:\n\n1. an object exposing `.profile` (the rebar-built providers: Anthropic, and Bedrock in S3) -> read it directly;\n2. a provider-qualified model **string** -> resolve the vendor's profile WITHOUT constructing a provider, via\n   a single `_PROFILE_RESOLVERS` map from provider name to the matching function in **`pydantic_ai.profiles.*`**;\n3. anything else -> the conservative record + one WARNING.\n\nThe critical verified fact that makes (2) work: **`pydantic_ai.profiles.*` are pure capability records and\nimport cleanly WITHOUT the vendor SDK** — `from pydantic_ai.profiles.openai import openai_model_profile`\nsucceeds and returns `supports_json_schema_output=True` with no `openai` package present, unlike\n`pydantic_ai.providers.openai`. So rebar reads capabilities for a provider it cannot construct, which is exactly\nthe asymmetry S1's lazy-string path needs.\n\n`_PROFILE_RESOLVERS` is a **single registry table**, and it is the one place a provider name maps to anything —\nthe same shape as `anthropic_model._PAI_PROVIDER_PREFIX`. The epic's criterion forbids *scattered*\nprovider-selection branches and requires name->behaviour mapping to live in one registry; this is that\nregistry, not a reintroduction of `startswith(\"anthropic\")` decision-making.\n\n### `supports_thinking`'s processing protocol — who reads it, and who does not\n\nThis field's consumer is **provenance, not control flow**, and that distinction is the whole protocol:\n\n- **`output_mode()` does NOT read it.** It keeps taking the caller-supplied `thinking: bool`, because pairing\n  extended thinking with a native/forced output constraint is a documented Anthropic **400 on the request** —\n  a property of the *call being made*, not of the model's capability. Reading the profile here would answer the\n  wrong question.\n- **S5 (`343b-4fdc-6722-469b`) reads it**, and reads it as part of the whole record: S5's signed provenance\n  payload is `{provider, model, endpoint_host, tier, capabilities}` where `capabilities` **is this record**. So\n  every field on this dataclass becomes part of a *signed, non-repudiable* verdict artifact.\n\nThat last point is why `supports_thinking` belongs on the record now rather than being deferred to the story\nthat first branches on it. rebar does **not** currently persist the model's chain of thought, so a verdict's\nreasoning is not recoverable after the fact. If chain-of-thought retention becomes a requirement — a plausible\nauditability ask for a gate that blocks merges — the signed record must already state whether the model that\nproduced each historical verdict was *capable* of thinking, because that fact cannot be reconstructed later\nfrom the sidecar. Adding the field after S5 ships is also strictly more expensive: it changes the shape of an\nalready-signed payload rather than being present from the first signature. The field is therefore load-bearing\nfor audit, and it is a deliberate, documented exception to \"no field without a consumer\" — the consumer is S5,\none story away in this same epic, and it is named here.\n\n### The rebar override table — schema, lookup, precedence, and its one entry\n\nThe override layer is **load-bearing, not speculative**, and the reason is concrete. Verified against pinned\npydantic-ai 1.107.1: `anthropic_model_profile(\"claude-opus-4-8\").supports_json_schema_output` is **`True`**, and\nso is Bedrock-hosted Claude's. A naive profile read would therefore set `native_structured_output=True` for\nClaude and flip `output_mode()` to `NativeOutput` — silently changing rebar's PRIMARY provider away from the\n`PromptedOutput` path that `structured.py:40-46` deliberately chose (pydantic-ai may still route strict\njson_schema through ToolOutput per profile, and PromptedOutput plus rebar's tolerant parse is the reliable\ncombination). `tests/unit/test_structured.py:179` already asserts Claude gets `PromptedOutput`. The override\ntable is what keeps that contract.\n\n**Schema.** An ordered tuple of `(predicate, overrides)` pairs — deliberately NOT a dict keyed by provider\nname, for two reasons: a name key would reintroduce exactly the string matching this story exists to remove,\nand it could not express the single rule below, which must span two different *hosts* of the same model family.\n\n```python\n_REBAR_OVERRIDES: tuple[tuple[Callable[[ModelProfile], bool], Mapping[str, object]], ...] = (\n    (_is_claude, {\"native_structured_output\": False}),\n)\n```\n\n- **Predicate**: `Callable[[ModelProfile], bool]`, reading the profile's **type** and/or its **capability\n  fields**. It never inspects the model string or the provider name.\n- **Value**: a partial mapping of `ModelCapabilities` field name -> value. Only the named fields are replaced;\n  unnamed fields keep their profile-derived value.\n- **Lookup**: iterate in declaration order; the **first** matching predicate wins and iteration stops. No\n  merging across multiple matches, so there is no precedence ambiguity to reason about.\n- **Precedence vs the profile**: overrides are applied **after** profile derivation (including after the\n  `prompt_cache_style` type-dispatch), so an override always wins over the upstream flag.\n\n**The one initial entry** identifies Claude on either host:\n\n```python\ndef _is_claude(profile: ModelProfile) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Direct-Anthropic OR Bedrock-hosted Anthropic.\"\"\"\n    return isinstance(profile, AnthropicModelProfile) or (\n        getattr(profile, \"bedrock_thinking_variant\", None) == \"anthropic\"\n    )\n```\n\nBoth arms are required and neither is redundant: **Bedrock-hosted Claude's profile is a `BedrockModelProfile`,\nNOT an `AnthropicModelProfile`** (verified — they are sibling `ModelProfile` subclasses), so an\n`isinstance(AnthropicModelProfile)` test alone would miss the very models this epic exists to support, and\nBedrock Claude would wrongly get `NativeOutput`. The second arm reads pydantic-ai's own\n`BedrockModelProfile.bedrock_thinking_variant` field, which is `'anthropic'` for Bedrock Claude, `'openai'` for\nBedrock-hosted OpenAI, and absent/`None` for Meta and Amazon models (all verified). Note this is a **capability\nfield read whose value happens to be a string enum** — materially different from\n`resolved.startswith(\"anthropic\")` on a model string, which is the pattern this story removes.\n\n**A rule derived purely from flags was tried and rejected.** `supports_json_schema_output and not\nsupports_thinking` looks elegant and reproduces Anthropic and OpenAI correctly, but it was tested against every\nprovider in today's `_NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS` and **breaks two**: `gemini-2.5-flash` has\n`supports_thinking=True` (would lose `NativeOutput`) and `groq` has `supports_json_schema_output=False` (same).\nSo `native_structured_output` is derived from `supports_json_schema_output` alone, plus this table.\n\n**One disclosed behavior change: groq.** `groq_model_profile(...).supports_json_schema_output` is `False`, yet\ntoday's hardcoded `_NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS` includes `groq`, so groq currently gets `NativeOutput` and after\nthis change gets `PromptedOutput`. This is accepted deliberately rather than papered over with a second\noverride entry: the profile is the authoritative capability source (the entire premise of this story), groq is\nnot reachable through rebar's own provider map (`_PAI_PROVIDER_PREFIX` covers anthropic/openai/google only), no\ntest asserts it, and the direction is the safe one — `PromptedOutput` works everywhere. A test pins the new\nbehavior so it is deliberate rather than incidental.\n\n### Cache-settings selection — ONE function, one shape\n\n`_anthropic_cache_settings(resolved: str)` is **deleted** from `anthropic_model.py` and replaced by a single\n`cache_settings_for(caps: ModelCapabilities)` in `capabilities.py`, which dispatches on\n`caps.prompt_cache_style`. This is a definite decision, not a choice left to the implementer: one function with\none calling convention, so `runner.py`'s call site has exactly one shape.\n\n**The exact settings keys** (verified against pinned pydantic-ai 1.107.1 — note\n`BedrockConverseModelSettings` does **not** exist; the class is `BedrockModelSettings`):\n\n| `prompt_cache_style` | class | keys set |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"anthropic\"` | `pydantic_ai.models.anthropic.AnthropicModelSettings` | `anthropic_cache_instructions=True`, `anthropic_cache_tool_definitions=True` |\n| `\"bedrock\"` | `pydantic_ai.models.bedrock.BedrockModelSettings` | `bedrock_cache_instructions=True`, `bedrock_cache_tool_definitions=True` |\n| `\"none\"` | — | returns `None` (no cache keys sent) |\n\n`AnthropicModelSettings` additionally has `anthropic_cache_messages` / `anthropic_cache` and\n`BedrockModelSettings` has `bedrock_cache_messages`; none of them are set here, preserving today's behavior\nexactly (only the instructions + tool-definitions breakpoints).\n\n### Degradation contract\n\nA model whose `.profile` is missing, `None`, or of an unrecognized type degrades to the **conservative** record\n(`native_structured_output=False`, `prompt_cache_style=\"none\"`) and logs **exactly one** WARNING naming the\nmodel type. Conservative because both fallbacks are the safe direction: `PromptedOutput` works everywhere, and\nsending no cache keys is always valid. It never raises — an unknown provider must not break a run.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** PR #121's `force_prompted` boolean. It is a per-call-site patch that grows one\nargument per capability per provider, and it encodes the answer at the caller rather than at the model — the\nnext provider needs another boolean threaded through the same four signatures.\n\n**Rollback.** The change is behavior-preserving for both currently-shipping providers, so backing out is a\nrevert of this single change; there is no data migration and no config flag to unwind. If a profile read\nmisbehaves in production the conservative fallback is already the safe state (prompted output, no cache keys),\nwhich degrades cost/latency but never correctness.\n\n## Scope\n\nProduction:\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` (**new** leaf) — `ModelCapabilities`, `capabilities_for()` (accepting a\n  model object OR a model string), the `_PROFILE_RESOLVERS` registry, the rebar override table, and\n  `cache_settings_for()`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/structured.py` — `output_mode()` (`:51`) takes a `ModelCapabilities` instead of a model\n  string; delete `_NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS` (`:46`) and its use (`:63`).\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — **three** edit targets:\n  - `:28` — the module-level `from rebar.llm.anthropic_model import (...)` block currently pulls in\n    `_anthropic_cache_settings`. Since that helper is deleted, this import must drop it and instead import\n    `cache_settings_for` (and `capabilities_for`) from `rebar.llm.capabilities`. Leaving it would be a hard\n    ImportError at module load, not a subtle failure.\n  - `:341` — the cache gate (the plan previously mis-cited `:316`, which is the toolset-filter line) reads\n    `prompt_cache_style` via `cache_settings_for()`.\n  - `:599` — the `output_mode()` call site passes the capability record instead of the model string.\n- `src/rebar/llm/anthropic_model.py` — **delete** `_anthropic_cache_settings` (`:39`) and its docstring\n  reference (`:10`).\n\nTests — **all four** affected files (S1 shipped with an under-enumerated test scope; this is the complete set,\nestablished by grepping every call site):\n\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_capabilities.py` (**new**) — the mapping + degradation + cache-settings selection +\n  `supports_thinking` derivation and its non-consumption by `output_mode()`.\n- `tests/unit/test_structured.py` (`:176`, `:177`, `:179`, `:189`) — four `output_mode()` calls using the old\n  string signature; must move to capability records.\n- `tests/unit/test_pydantic_ai_runner.py` — **both** cache tests, not just one:\n  `test_cache_settings_enabled_only_for_anthropic` (`:310`, plus the direct `_anthropic_cache_settings` import\n  at `:17` and its four calls at `:313`–`:319`) and\n  `test_cache_model_settings_attached_only_for_anthropic` (`:358`).\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_execution_mode_dispatch.py` (`:90`) — monkeypatches\n  `runner_mod._anthropic_cache_settings`; that patch target disappears, so it must move to the new seam. (This\n  fourth site is the same file whose `_build_retrying_anthropic_model` patch S1 had to fix — it stubs\n  `pydantic_ai` empty, so its replacement must not force a real settings-class import.)\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tier for the mapping (a pure function over a profile) plus contract tier for the two consumers, since the\ndefect class is a consumer reading the wrong signal.\n\n- happy path: an Anthropic model yields `prompt_cache_style == \"anthropic\"` and `output_mode` returns\n  `PromptedOutput`; an OpenAI model yields `native_structured_output=True` and `NativeOutput`.\n- **Bedrock case (the motivating defect):** a `BedrockConverseModel` carrying a `BedrockModelProfile` yields\n  `prompt_cache_style == \"bedrock\"`, and `cache_settings_for()` returns a `BedrockModelSettings` whose\n  `bedrock_cache_instructions` and `bedrock_cache_tool_definitions` are both `True` — i.e. caching is ON for\n  Bedrock-hosted Claude, which is exactly what silently switched off before. The Bedrock model is constructed\n  offline (no AWS call, no credentials) since only its profile is read.\n- edge: `thinking=True` forces `PromptedOutput` regardless of profile (existing contract, must not regress).\n- failure path: a model whose profile is missing/`None`/unrecognized degrades to the conservative record\n  (`native_structured_output=False`, `prompt_cache_style=\"none\"`) rather than raising, and logs exactly one\n  WARNING (asserted with `caplog`, including that it is one record, not per-call spam).\n- contrast case: `test_cache_model_settings_attached_only_for_anthropic` is rewritten to assert by capability\n  rather than by provider name, and still shows cache settings absent for a non-caching model.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `grep -rn '_NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS' src/ tests/` is empty\n- [ ] `grep -rn 'force_prompted' src/ tests/` is empty — no `force_prompted`-style boolean is added to any\n      signature (this is the proving command)\n- [ ] `grep -rn '_anthropic_cache_settings' src/ tests/` is empty — the string-gated helper is gone, replaced\n      by the single `cache_settings_for()` seam\n- [ ] `output_mode()` accepts a `ModelCapabilities` record, and its tests assert `NativeOutput` for a native\n      provider, `PromptedOutput` for Anthropic, and the `thinking=True` override\n- [ ] a test asserts a Bedrock-hosted Claude model yields `prompt_cache_style == \"bedrock\"` and that\n      `cache_settings_for()` returns `BedrockModelSettings` with `bedrock_cache_instructions` and\n      `bedrock_cache_tool_definitions` set — the Bedrock cache path has an executable proof, constructed\n      offline with no AWS credentials\n- [ ] a test asserts an unknown/missing profile produces the conservative record AND exactly one WARNING log\n      record\n- [ ] `supports_thinking` is on the record, derived from `ModelProfile.supports_thinking`, asserted `True` for a\n      thinking-capable Anthropic model and `False` in the conservative fallback — and a test asserts\n      `output_mode()` does NOT consult it (a thinking-capable model with `thinking=False` still gets\n      `NativeOutput` when its profile supports json_schema), so the provenance field cannot silently become a\n      control-flow input\n- [ ] the override table is an ordered `(predicate, overrides)` tuple whose predicates read only profile TYPE\n      and profile FIELDS — `grep -n 'startswith' src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` is empty — and a test asserts\n      **both** arms of the Claude rule: a direct `AnthropicModelProfile` AND a Bedrock-hosted Claude profile\n      (a `BedrockModelProfile` with `bedrock_thinking_variant == \"anthropic\"`) each yield\n      `native_structured_output=False`, while Bedrock-hosted Meta/Amazon profiles do NOT match the rule\n- [ ] a test pins the disclosed groq change (`PromptedOutput` where it was `NativeOutput`), so it is deliberate\n      rather than an accidental regression, and tests confirm `openai` and `google-gla` still get `NativeOutput`\n- [ ] `capabilities_for()` accepts a model STRING as well as a model object, and a test asserts that a lazy\n      `\"openai:gpt-4o\"` string (the form S1's runner leaves for a provider rebar does not build) still yields\n      `native_structured_output=True` — proving the profile is read WITHOUT the `openai` SDK installed, and\n      that the conservative fallback is NOT reached for a provider rebar merely defers\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` imports nothing from `runner` or `providers`, and `providers.py` stays\n      under the 300 LOC that S1's attested criterion fixed\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the FULL gating suite (`make test`, not just `tests/unit`) pass"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785363698893915001,"uuid":"25e9230c-6668-4a02-bc33-1920d6ba7825"}